+# Implementation Plan: Recognized Signal Retry Returns Original Signal Id
+
+**Branch**: `main` | **Date**: 2026-10-27 | **Spec**: [spec.md](spec.md)
+
+**Input**: Feature specification from `/specs/001-bench/spec.md`
+
+**Note**: This template is filled in by the `__SPECKIT_COMMAND_PLAN__` command; its definition describes the execution workflow.
+
+## Summary
+
+The public management API's signal endpoint (`POST /v1/executions/{id}/signal`) already recognises idempotent retries of an already-committed signal — same `Idempotency-Key`, same body, same authenticated actor — and answers 200 with the current execution status plus an additive `idempotency` retry-proof block (`recognizedRetry: true`, `originalDecisionSequence`). That recognised-retry reply does **not** carry the original durable `signalId` the first submission's reply did, so a client retrying after a lost acknowledgement cannot correlate its own submission with the durable record.
+
+This feature makes the recognised signal-retry reply additionally carry the original `signalId`, recovered verbatim from the originally committed `signal_recorded` decision event and placed where the fresh signal reply already places it (`data.signalId`). Refusals (mismatched actor, wrong decision kind), fresh submissions, and every other mutation kind's recognised-retry reply stay byte-for-byte unchanged. No new storage, endpoint, diagnostic code, or dependency is introduced. The existing end-to-end HTTP suite (`apps/cli/tests/api_http.rs`) gains one regression scenario asserting FR-001 and FR-003/FR-004 that fails against pre-change code (SC-004).
+
+Technical approach (full reasoning in [research.md](research.md)): the serve layer's idempotency classification (`classify_existing_keys` in `apps/cli/src/commands/serve/mod.rs`) already scans the execution's entire raw history once per request and returns the committed sequence of the first server-derived decision key — today rendered as `originalDecisionSequence`. The change captures, in that same single scan, the `SignalRecorded.signal_id` of the committed decision event whenever that event is a `signal_recorded`, threads it through `KeyState::Complete` into `reply_with_current_status` → `annotate_recognized_retry`, and inserts `data.signalId` when the request's command is the signal command. Retrieval is a pure read of committed bytes; the retry path opens no new stores and scans no history it did not already scan.
+
+## Technical Context
+
+**Language/Version**: Rust 1.97.1 (pinned toolchain per AGENTS.md; all commands run through `cargo +1.97.1 ...`).
+
+**Primary Dependencies**: none new. The change uses only what `graphhelm-cli` already depends on: `axum` (response types), `serde_json`, and workspace crates `graphhelm-events` / `graphhelm-protocols` (event envelopes and the `SignalRecorded` payload type).
+
+**Storage**: the local append-only file-based event store (`LocalEventRepository`), read through the existing `resolve_stream` history load. No layout or schema change: the signal identifier is already durably bound into the committed `signal_recorded` event payload (`SignalRecorded.signal_id`, wire name `signalId`).
+
+**Testing**: `cargo test -p graphhelm-cli --test api_http --locked` (the existing end-to-end HTTP integration suite in `apps/cli/tests/api_http.rs`) plus the serve layer's in-process unit tests inside `apps/cli/src/commands/serve/mod.rs` (`mod tests`). The suite's harness already provides fixed clocks/IDs (`WallClock`, `Ids::default()`), an in-process `serve()` child-process guard on loopback, and reusable helpers (`cli_start`, `signal_envelope`, `post_json`, `head_sequence`, `last_event_of_kind`, `append_wrong_decision_kind`).
+
+**Target Platform**: Windows PowerShell first (per AGENTS.md all commands must work there); the touched crate compiles and tests cross-platform today, and nothing platform-specific is added.
+
+**Project Type**: CLI with an embedded local HTTP management API (`graphhelm serve`, in `apps/cli`).
+
+**Performance Goals**: N/A as a throughput target — local control plane behind one bearer token. Constraint instead: the recognised-retry path adds no new store open and no new history scan beyond what classification and status read already perform.
+
+**Constraints**:
+- The JSON envelope (`{ ok, command, data, diagnostics }`) remains the machine-readable contract for non-terminal consumers (AGENTS.md, D-056/#1172). This change is purely additive inside `data` of one reply variant.
+- No new stable diagnostic codes; the defensive failure arm reuses the existing idempotency-reply code at path `/data`. Refusals stay non-disclosing (no original actor or identifier in refused replies).
+- No network, model, provider SDK, shell, or browser involvement anywhere on the touched paths; tests use loopback only.
+- Recovery must be pure and deterministic over committed store bytes (FR-002: verbatim, never re-derived from the retry request).
+- English-only documentation (AGENTS.md documentation rule).
+
+**Scale/Scope**: two files change:
+- `apps/cli/src/commands/serve/mod.rs` — targeted edits in the idempotency classification/reply construction (`KeyState::Complete`, `classify_one_key`/`classify_existing_keys`, `reply_with_current_status`, `reply_with_status_value`, `annotate_recognized_retry`).
+- `apps/cli/tests/api_http.rs` — one new regression test function (plus, at most, additive absence assertions in existing refusal tests; existing assertions are not edited, per SC-003).
+
+Read-only references: `apps/cli/src/commands/serve/routes.rs` (the `POST .../signal` route), `apps/cli/src/commands/execution/signal.rs` (fresh reply shape — the naming/location precedent), `core/protocols/src/event.rs` (`SignalRecorded`). No core-crate, schema, example, or Studio changes.
+
+## Constitution Check
+
+*GATE: Must pass before Phase 0 research. Re-check after Phase 1 design.*
+
+**Governance reference — substitution recorded**: `/memory/constitution.md` does not exist in this checkout. Per this run's stage setup, the repository-root **AGENTS.md** is used as the governance reference in its place (also recorded in spec.md's Assumptions). The gates below are drawn from AGENTS.md's constitutional architecture invariants, security rules, Journey-Proven Development proof rules, and Keel proportionality.
+
