+# Feature Specification: Recognized Signal Retry Returns Original Signal Id
+
+**Feature Branch**: N/A (no `before_specify` hook is registered in this checkout, so no branch was created in this run)
+
+**Created**: 2026-10-27
+
+**Status**: Draft
+
+**Input**: User description: "`POST /v1/executions/{id}/signal` answers a recognised idempotent retry (same key, same body, same actor) with the current status and an `idempotency` block, but without the `signalId` that the first reply carried, so a client retrying after a lost acknowledgement cannot match it (`apps/cli/src/commands/serve/mod.rs`, `reply_with_current_status`). Return the original durable `signalId` on a recognised retry of `execution.signal`; a mismatched actor or a wrong-kind event must still get no retry proof and no `signalId`. Add the regression assertion to the existing HTTP tests in `apps/cli/tests/api_http.rs`; it must fail without your change."
+
+## User Scenarios & Testing *(mandatory)*
+
+### User Story 1 - Client recovers its signal identity after a lost acknowledgement (Priority: P1)
+
+A client (typically an agent) submits a signal about an execution and chooses an idempotency key so the submission can be safely repeated. The first acknowledgement is lost in transit — a timeout, a dropped connection, or a crashed process between send and receive. The client re-sends the byte-identical request: same idempotency key, same body (including the client-chosen signal identifier), same authenticated actor. The control plane recognises that this mutation already happened and answers with the current status plus a retry proof that identifies the original committed decision. Today that reply omits the signal identifier the first reply carried, so the retrier cannot correlate "the signal I sent" with "the signal that was durably recorded". After this change, the recognised retry carries the original durable signal identifier back to the caller, making the retry indistinguishable-in-effect from having received the first acknowledgement.
+
+**Why this priority**: This is the core value of the feature. Without it, a client that must retry a signal submission (exactly the unreliable-transport situation idempotency keys exist for) cannot reliably match its own submission against the durable record, forcing workarounds (extra polling, local guesswork) or duplicates-by-accident.
+
+**Independent Test**: Fully testable by driving the signal endpoint end-to-end over the public API: submit a signal, re-send it identically, and verify the second response contains the retry proof *and* the same signal identifier the first response contained. Delivers the full user value on its own.
+
+**Acceptance Scenarios**:
+
+1. **Given** a signal was successfully committed under an idempotency key by an actor, and the stream has advanced with later unrelated activity, **When** the same actor re-submits the identical idempotency key and body, **Then** the reply is a success, carries the recognised-retry proof naming the original decision, and carries the original signal identifier exactly as the first reply did — even though newer events now exist.
+2. **Given** a signal was committed under an idempotency key by actor A, **When** actor B submits the same idempotency key and byte-identical body, **Then** the request is refused as an unrecognised retry: no retry proof, no signal identifier in the reply, and nothing new is appended to the execution record.
+3. **Given** an idempotency key already resolves to a committed event that is validly formed but of a different decision kind than a signal, **When** a signal submission arrives under that key, **Then** the request is refused: no retry proof, no signal identifier, and nothing new is appended.
+
+---
+
+### User Story 2 - First-time submissions and other commands are untouched (Priority: P2)
+
+Existing callers of the signal endpoint (and of every other mutation endpoint) rely on today's response shapes: a fresh signal submission returns its signal identifier and never a retry marker; other mutation commands' recognised-retry replies keep exactly their current contents. This change must be purely additive to the recognised signal-retry reply, so no existing consumer sees altered behaviour outside that one case.
+
+**Why this priority**: The second most important property of the change is that it breaks nothing. It guards against a fix that, for example, starts stamping signal identifiers onto retry replies of other commands or onto refused requests.
+
+**Independent Test**: Testable by running the pre-existing end-to-end API test scenarios for fresh signals, recognised and refused retries, and other mutation kinds: every assertion that held before must hold unchanged, and the new field must appear nowhere except in the recognised signal-retry reply.
+
+**Acceptance Scenarios**:
+
+1. **Given** a never-before-seen idempotency key, **When** a signal is submitted, **Then** the reply is a fresh success carrying the signal identifier and no retry marker, exactly as before.
+2. **Given** a recognised retry reply for a signal, **When** the same request is repeated once more, **Then** both recognised-retry replies carry the identical original signal identifier and otherwise behave as today's contract requires.
+3. **Given** a recognised retry of a different, non-signal mutation kind, **When** it is answered, **Then** its reply shape is byte-for-byte the established shape for that command — no signal identifier field appears.
+
+---
+
+### Edge Cases
+
+- **Stream advanced far past the original decision.** The retried signal's identifier must be recovered from the originally committed decision record, not derived from whatever event happens to sit at the current head.
+- **Multiple retries of the same key.** Every recognised retry returns the same original signal identifier, consistently.
+- **Refusals leak nothing.** An actor mismatch or wrong-kind refusal must not disclose the original signal identifier (nor the original actor) in its reply, matching today's non-disclosure behaviour for conflict refusals.
+- **The retry-proof identity is untouched.** The recognised-retry proof continues to identify the original decision by its committed sequence only; the new identifier rides beside it as additional proof, never replacing or altering it.
+- **Identifier naming consistency.** The field uses the same name and location in the reply data as the fresh signal reply already uses for the same value, so a client needs one rule ("read the signal identifier from the reply") regardless of whether it saw the first attempt or a retry.
+
+## Requirements *(mandatory)*
+
+### Functional Requirements
+
+- **FR-001**: On a recognised idempotent retry of a signal submission (same idempotency key, same request body, same authenticated actor), the system MUST return the original durable signal identifier that the first successful submission returned.
+- **FR-002**: The returned signal identifier MUST be the value bound into the originally committed decision record, recovered verbatim — never recomputed, re-derived, or echoed from the retry request.
+- **FR-003**: When the retry cannot be authenticated because the presenting actor differs from the committing actor, the system MUST return no retry proof and no signal identifier, and MUST refuse the request exactly as it does today.
+- **FR-004**: When the idempotency key resolves to a committed event of a different decision kind than a signal, the system MUST return no retry proof and no signal identifier, and MUST refuse the request exactly as it does today.
+- **FR-005**: Fresh (first-time) signal submissions MUST keep their exact current reply behaviour: they carry the signal identifier and no retry marker.
+- **FR-006**: All other parts of the recognised signal-retry reply — current status, the retry proof naming the original decision, and the current head position — MUST be unchanged by this feature.
+- **FR-007**: Recognised-retry replies of every mutation kind other than the signal MUST be unchanged.
+- **FR-008**: The existing end-to-end API test suite MUST gain a regression scenario asserting FR-001 and FR-003/FR-004, written so that it fails against the pre-change behaviour and passes against the fixed behaviour (the assertion must observe the new field, not merely freeze unrelated shape).
+
+### Key Entities *(include if feature involves data)*
+
+- **Signal submission request**: the caller's intent to record a signal — its chosen idempotency key, its body (which includes the caller-chosen signal identifier), and the authenticated actor presenting it.
+- **Committed signal decision record**: the durable, append-only record proving a signal happened; it durably binds the signal identifier to the idempotency key, the actor, and a monotonically increasing decision position. It is the single source of truth for "what did the original attempt record".
+- **Recognised-retry reply**: the response given to a correctly authenticated repeat of an already-committed mutation — current status, the retry proof (original decision position), and, for signals only, now additionally the original signal identifier.
+
+## Success Criteria *(mandatory)*
+
+### Measurable Outcomes
+
+- **SC-001**: On 100% of recognised signal retries, the reply contains the original signal identifier, byte-identical to the value the first submission's reply carried — verifiable by the new end-to-end regression scenario comparing the two replies directly.
+- **SC-002**: On 100% of refused signal retries (actor mismatch and wrong-kind evidence), the reply contains neither a retry proof nor a signal identifier.
+- **SC-003**: 100% of pre-existing end-to-end API expectations for fresh signals, recognised and refused retries, and all other mutation kinds continue to pass with zero assertion edits outside the additions this feature calls for.
+- **SC-004**: The new regression scenario demonstrably fails when the change is reverted and passes when applied — i.e., it observes the fixed behaviour rather than passing vacuously.
+
+## Assumptions
+
+- **The signal identifier is durable**: it is part of the committed decision record and is itself checked when authenticating a retry (a retry only matches if the committed record's identifier equals the request's), so it is recoverable at retry time from the record alone. The change therefore needs no new storage — only to surface the already-bound value.
+- **Placement and naming follow the existing convention**: the identifier is surfaced in the recognised-retry reply using the same field name and the same position in the reply data that a fresh signal reply uses, keeping one client-side rule.
+- **Scope is the signal mutation only**, at the public management API endpoint for submitting signals (the `POST /v1/executions/{id}/signal` contract named in the input). Retries of other mutation kinds getting analogous original-identifier recovery is explicitly out of scope.
+- **Conflict refusals keep today's diagnostics and non-disclosure semantics**: no new error codes, no disclosure of the original actor or identifier.
+- **Constitution substitution**: no `/memory/constitution.md` exists in this checkout; per the run's stage setup, the repository-root `AGENTS.md` serves as the governance reference. Its constraints relevant here: the JSON envelope stays the machine-readable contract for non-terminal consumers; the stable-diagnostics convention (code + path) is unchanged because no new diagnostics are introduced; documentation, including this spec, is written in English.
+- **Unattended-run assumptions**: no `before_specify` hook is registered (`.specify/extensions.yml` absent), so no git branch was created; the feature directory is the fixed `specs/001-bench` from the stage setup rather than an auto-generated name.
